WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.901
-Thanks so much for coming back to the show.

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:04.267
-Are you kidding? I love being here.

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:05.367
-We love having you here.

00:00:05.367 --> 00:00:06.467
And look at this. On the cover of "Elle."

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:07.601
Looking gorgeous as always.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:10.534
They call you "the legend," by the way.

00:00:10.534 --> 00:00:12.501
-Does it say that? -"The legend."

00:00:12.501 --> 00:00:15.434
-I thought it was "a legend." -Yeah.

00:00:15.434 --> 00:00:17.534
-I didn't know I was "the legend."

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:20.000
-Of course. "The" legend.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.501
-I haven't had the cards made up.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.000
-That's got to feel good, though, right?

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.501
Do you feel like a legend? -Well, I'll tell you.

00:00:25.501 --> 00:00:28.367
I wondered if it had any practical application, you know?

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:32.334
Like, would I still be able to wear sweatpants?

00:00:32.334 --> 00:00:33.734
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:00:33.734 --> 00:00:35.467
-Would this help me get out of emptying the dishwasher?

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.367
-No. A legend doesn't --

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:39.801
-So I'm still waiting to find out.

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:41.267
-Yeah, the legendary status.

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:43.701
I was looking through your credits, as well,

00:00:43.701 --> 00:00:45.234
just to see everything.

00:00:45.234 --> 00:00:48.000
You've just been in so many epic movies, and it's just insane.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.000
And I was looking through this.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.334
You've been Oscar-nominated three times,

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:54.801
Emmy-nominated four times, Tony-nominated.

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:56.334
You won two Golden Globes.

00:00:56.334 --> 00:00:58.033
I think it kind of makes you a legend.

00:00:58.033 --> 00:00:59.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:01.367
Yeah. -Yeah.

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:03.701
-I think it makes you a legend. Yeah.

00:01:03.701 --> 00:01:05.667
-It's exciting.

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:07.634
-Legends are always welcome here.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:09.801
-Thank you. I think if you last long enough,

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:11.534
someone will give you a "legend."

00:01:11.534 --> 00:01:13.868
-You're so cool. You're too cool.

00:01:13.868 --> 00:01:16.300
Every time you come, I always want to talk about

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.667
and say thanks to your dad

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:20.834
because Pat Weaver -- without your dad,

00:01:20.834 --> 00:01:23.267
thinking up the idea of "Today" show and "Tonight Show,"

00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:25.501
which is true, I wouldn't have a job.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:27.400
So thank you very much. I always thank you.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.767
I want to put it out there. [ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:33.133
-He'd be so excited that the show is so wonderful

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.501
and so many people enjoy it all over the country.

00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:37.868
-It's wild. I wish -- You know, I do --

00:01:37.868 --> 00:01:39.801
I think about -- I do think about your dad.

00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:42.167
I think about Steve Allen and Johnny Carson,

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:45.367
and I go, like, "I wish that they could see how big

00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:46.801
the show is now because..." -Yeah.

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:49.133
-Even through the Internet and streaming now, the --

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:50.467
It's worldwide. It's global.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:52.567
-That's true. It's a much bigger party than it was.

00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:54.767
-Is this what got you into show business?

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:56.400
Like, coming to the "Today" show

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:58.601
and seeing your dad in show business?

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:00.400
-Well, I did used to come to the "Today" show,

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.200
not "The Tonight Show," because I had an early bedtime.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.667
-Yeah, you were little.

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:09.934
-Yeah, I used to come, and there was that nasty chimp,

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:11.634
J. Fred Muggs.

00:02:11.634 --> 00:02:13.934
-Wait. There was a monkey that was the mascot.

00:02:13.934 --> 00:02:15.400
-Yeah, a little monkey in clothes,

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.868
which is not good for them. -Yeah, he would wear --

00:02:18.868 --> 00:02:20.434
-He was the mascot for the "Today" show?

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:22.067
-He wasn't a mascot.

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:25.834
He became, like, the other person at the table.

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:27.467
They needed something

00:02:27.467 --> 00:02:29.667
to make the show more entertaining, I guess.

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:31.467
-And you would come see and you hang out with him?

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:33.868
-No, I would not hang out with him.

00:02:33.868 --> 00:02:35.334
I was afraid of him.

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:38.000
The first time I met him, he leaped off the desk,

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.167
onto my shoulders, took my little hat

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:43.868
that only went with this dress-up coat,

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:45.501
and ran down the hallway with it

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:48.267
and tore it into shreds. I never saw it again.

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:50.434
-I mean, he's an animal. That's crazy.

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:51.934
-It amazing I was able to do

00:02:51.934 --> 00:02:53.367
"Gorillas in the Mist" so many years later.

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:55.868
-Exactly. You were traumatized.

00:02:55.868 --> 00:02:58.000
But you would come here and go like,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.434
"Wait. What is show business?

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:01.133
I don't understand what this job is."

00:03:01.133 --> 00:03:03.868
-Well, I remember, you know,

00:03:03.868 --> 00:03:07.767
he didn't bring us into the network, you know.

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:09.367
He was doing "Show of Shows," too,

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:11.267
and all these different things. -Wow.

00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:15.167
-But he used to come home with a smile on his face

00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:17.267
and very lighthearted.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:21.834
So, I mean, live TV was, I imagine, quite stressful,

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:24.601
but he seemed to be hanging out with great people,

00:03:24.601 --> 00:03:26.100
and he loved to laugh.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.100
My father had a laugh. Just he would -- It would --

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.000
He would just not be able to stop laughing when he laughed.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.133
And I thought, "Well, show business, maybe it's fun."

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:38.834
-Yeah, I think it actually is. -Yeah.

00:03:38.834 --> 00:03:40.801
-Turned out -- Thank gosh you got into show business.

00:03:40.801 --> 00:03:42.567
-It is all about the people. -It is fun, yeah.

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:45.267
I know we probably can't talk about much about the --

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:47.234
But I know you're in the biggest-grossing

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:48.601
movie of all time, "Avatar,"

00:03:48.601 --> 00:03:50.834
and they just made -- No big deal.

00:03:50.834 --> 00:03:53.167
[ Cheers and applause ] No big deal.

00:03:53.167 --> 00:03:56.534
Add to the legend. Add to the legend.

00:03:56.534 --> 00:03:58.567
well, there's a sequel that's coming up.

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:00.667
There's another sequel. We can't even --

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:02.434
Can we talk about what it's about

00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:04.167
or if it's coming? Are you --

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:06.300
-There are going to be four more.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.968
This is "Avatar 2" coming out in December.

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:13.667
We shot "2" and "3" together a few years ago now.

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:17.267
And I play a 14-year-old girl.

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:19.267
-Yes.

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:22.000
-Of course. -Yeah. Why wouldn't you? Yeah.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.067
-I was delighted to be offered that job

00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:25.801
because I had never done anything like that.

00:04:25.801 --> 00:04:27.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.801
And I dare say John Wayne was never asked to play

00:04:29.801 --> 00:04:31.133
a 14-year-old girl.

00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:34.367
-Is it fun -- That must be -- Yeah, that must be fun

00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:36.767
to get into your 14-year-old self.

00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:38.567
Do you think back to when you were 14?

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:40.968
-Were you a happy 14-year-old?

00:04:40.968 --> 00:04:44.100
[ Laughter ] -I mean, I probably was.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.734
I mean, I think I had a pretty happy life.

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:49.167
I was a happy-go-lucky kid. -That's good.

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.734
-A lot of 14-year-olds, yeah, it's a tough couple years.

00:04:51.734 --> 00:04:54.734
-Puberty. I mean, I --

00:04:54.734 --> 00:04:57.667
I was this tall when I was 11. -Were you really?

00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:01.033
-I was like this. "Hi." -Oh, no. Oh, no.

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.067
-I was very shy. I was very self-conscious.

00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:07.467
So I did, of course, try to channel my --

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:13.000
my very self-conscious, awkward, you know, teenager.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.968
And I think actually that's what Jim wanted,

00:05:15.968 --> 00:05:18.868
although he said when he offered me the part,

00:05:18.868 --> 00:05:21.067
he said, "You know, she's a 14-year-old girl,

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:23.033
but that's about how old you are anyway

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:25.434
because you're such a goof ball. You can do this."

00:05:25.434 --> 00:05:28.300
-Yeah. I love that. In this movie, you had to learn

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.634
how to, like, hold your breath for a long time.

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:32.534
-We did. -A lot of it's underwater.

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:35.501
-Yeah, we had to learn to free dive, and the first thing --

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.501
And we had this amazing teacher named Kirk Krack

00:05:38.501 --> 00:05:40.834
who teaches the Navy SEALs.

00:05:40.834 --> 00:05:42.801
So we had him for about a year

00:05:42.801 --> 00:05:45.834
training us to do breath holds,

00:05:45.834 --> 00:05:50.167
and, you know, I actually got up to six and a half minutes.

00:05:50.167 --> 00:05:51.934
[ Audience ohhs ]

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:53.934
[ Cheers and applause ] Legend.

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:57.634
-Legend, legend, legend. That's how you become one.

00:05:57.634 --> 00:06:00.167
How do you -- -No, no, no, but I --

00:06:00.167 --> 00:06:02.133
-Do you freak out? -I was not moving.

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:04.667
It's much easier to hold your breath when you're --

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:06.701
-I can't hold my breath for 30 seconds.

00:06:06.701 --> 00:06:08.734
-But you could if Kirk Krack taught you.

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:10.601
-What is the secret? Can you tell us?

00:06:10.601 --> 00:06:15.234
-The secret is training up so that you --

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:18.601
The urge to breathe is a natural instinct.

00:06:18.601 --> 00:06:22.033
So when you're trying to just keep a breath hold --

00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:24.601
Of course, I also had enhanced oxygen.

00:06:24.601 --> 00:06:26.601
This is 30% oxygen,

00:06:26.601 --> 00:06:29.667
and I had like, you know, 50% or 60% oxygen.

00:06:29.667 --> 00:06:33.267
Anyway, when the desire to breathe comes,

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:35.000
he called it "the dirty villain."

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.734
And you ignore the dirty villain.

00:06:36.734 --> 00:06:39.234
And you go, "Ha! You, go away!"

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:41.267
And then you try to do all these mind games.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:45.133
Like, I used to paint myself blue, you know, pretend,

00:06:45.133 --> 00:06:47.601
and then I'd sing, "Speed Bunny Boat,"

00:06:47.601 --> 00:06:49.667
like, you know, "Bird on the Wing."

00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:51.734
Anything you can do to keep from thinking about,

00:06:51.734 --> 00:06:53.167
"I really want to breathe."

00:06:53.167 --> 00:06:54.534
-No way. -Yeah.

00:06:54.534 --> 00:06:56.601
-So if you're in trouble, you've got to go --

00:06:56.601 --> 00:06:59.100
What do you do? They have things --

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:00.634
-Well, I think we have --

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:03.701
We had a lot of safety. The biggest challenge, frankly --

00:07:03.701 --> 00:07:06.100
Because "Avatar 2" takes place

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.534
a lot in the water and around the water.

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:11.367
But what Jim asked us to do, which was, I thought, hard,

00:07:11.367 --> 00:07:15.868
was when you're underneath the water, you can't go...

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.234
You know?

00:07:17.234 --> 00:07:22.434
You have to pretend you're so happy. You have to go...

00:07:22.434 --> 00:07:24.834
-That's what I'm talking about. -Yeah.

00:07:24.834 --> 00:07:26.901
That's good acting. I'm going to practice.

00:07:26.901 --> 00:07:28.100
Next time you come back,

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.968
I will show you my underwater acting.

00:07:29.968 --> 00:07:31.234
More with Sigourney Weaver.

00:07:31.234 --> 00:07:32.901
I want to talk about your new movie, "Call Jane,"

00:07:32.901 --> 00:07:34.801
when we come back, everybody.

